            <div xml:id="d717">

                <pb facs="0613" n="601" sameAs="frus:frus1942v04/pg601"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">890D.01/615: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-07-18T14:00:00+02:00">July 18, 1942—2
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received July 18—noon.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3975. Personal for the Secretary. Your message number 3272, July 15, 9 p.m. After
                    making inquiry, I have decided it best to take this matter up with Mr. Eden
                    which I shall do on Monday. General Spears was Mr. Churchill’s military contact
                    officer with the French during the time when Mr. Churchill was Prime Minister
                    and the French nation was still engaged on the European front. He is also an old
                    personal friend. Because of other matters that are under consideration here at
                    this time, I hope you will give me a little time to try to work out a
                    satisfactory solution.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Winant</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
